// A writer other than os.Stdout / os.Stderr must be a clean compile error, not
// a write to whatever file descriptor the integer happened to name.
//
// The fmt.Fprint family types its writer slot int64 (a descriptor) because v1
// has no io.Writer. This guard is what makes that safe: no program can name
// the fd, so none can observe or forge it. Without the guard,
// fmt.Fprintln(3, "x") would type-check and write to fd 3.
//
// Widening this to a real writer is blocked on interface-in-struct embedding,
// which Goo rejects today — not on a decision here.
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	fmt.Fprintln(3, "goes nowhere good")
}
